A||||4||Greg Piet||Valve lash especially with new lifters||||1||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||01-15-2000||02:30 AM||||Well i had a nasty knock after my v6 redlined a few times due to a stuck accel pedal.  I was hoping it was a lifter, so off to engine land I went.  I replaced the lifters, but while reading on the proper procedure for adjusting valve lash, I was a bit unclear as to exactly what should be done. By the way the knock is gone.<P>Firstly my old lifters that were not blown were nearly impossible to depress, but the new ones after my attempt of priming them would fairly easily depress for about 1/8th of an inch.  Should i be trying to rotate the pushrods and when I cant rotate the pushrod tighten another 1.5 turns?<P>Also since I had this 1/8th of an inch I could easily depress on the new lifter, should I redo the lash now that the motor has run for about an hour and all the microbubbles in the lifter should be gone?<P>After getting my landlord all miffed at me for taking apart the motor in the parking lot I dont want to mess up the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-15-2000||03:52 AM||||It should only be 1/2 to 3/4 turn after the pushrod slack is gone, just enought to keep it from rattling when cold.  If the valve in the lifter moves freely then the adjustment should stay correct and not need readjusted.  Unless you want to zero-lash the valves for overrevving the cam's recommended limit, after completely warming up the engine remover the valve covers, start the engine back up and loosen the rockers one at a time while trying to spin the pushrod with your fingers (yes, it is going up and down and is hot) give it about a sixteenth turn, just enough to make the pushrod firm.  Now there may be a slight rattle when cold but a lot less chance of valve float when pushing for that last bit of power or missing a shift.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||01-15-2000||08:49 AM||||The reason you can move the new lifters some and not the old ones is because the old ones are pumped up with oil. New lifters should put into a container and covered with fresh engine oil overnight. This allows oil to enter the lifter through the little bleeder holes so they're not completely dry when you install them. Lifters installed without soaking in oil sometimes need to be readjusted later on. What I usually do to a new engine is to roughly set the rocker arms then after I start the engine, make sure I have oil pressure, get the timing adjusted, etc., I then go back and double check the valve adjustment one last time before I button it all back up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FIEROCIOUS||07-17-2000||04:51 PM||||How do you take the valve covers off without removeing the intake? is it even posible?||||reg||1
Z||000004||pollock||07-18-2000||09:38 AM||||Not trying to nitpick, but it's not till you can't "rotate" the pushrod, but instead just take up all the linear slack.  If you tighten it down enough to not be able to rotate it, you're in a heap of trouble.||||reg||1
